  "raw_text": "Step 1: Identify the possible rational roots using the Rational Root Theorem. According to the theorem, any rational root of the polynomial \\(2x^4 + a_3 x^3 + a_2 x^2 + a_1 x + 1 = 0\\) must be of the form \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\), where \\(p\\) is a factor of the constant term (1) and \\(q\\) is a factor of the leading coefficient (2).\n\nStep 2: List all factors of the constant term 1. The factors are \\(\\pm 1\\).\n\nStep 3: List all factors of the leading coefficient 2. The factors are \\(\\pm 1, \\pm 2\\).\n\nStep 4: Form all possible rational roots by taking each factor of the constant term divided by each factor of the leading coefficient. This gives us the possible rational roots: \\(\\pm 1, \\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nStep 5: Count the number of different possible rational roots. There are 4 different possible rational roots: \\(1, -1, \\frac{1}{2}, -\\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{4}<|im_end|>",
